: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\n- `nextGeneration` — accurately describes computing the next board state from live cells. **Keep.**\n- `toKey` — converts coordinates to a string map key. Clear. **Keep.**\n- `NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS`, `liveCells`, `liveNeighborCounts`, `result` — all reveal intent. **Keep.**\n- **Gap found:** The core Conway rule lives as an inline boolean expression `count === 3 || (isAlive && count === 2)`. This is the heart of the algorithm but has no name explaining *why* those numbers matter. Extracting a named predicate `survivesOrIsBorn(isAlive, liveNeighbors)` would reveal intent (Rule 2).\n\nAlso, `cellKey.split(\",\").map(Number)` reconstructs coordinates from strings — a fragile inverse of `toKey`. We can avoid the round-trip by storing the coordinate alongside the count, removing the parse step entirely (Rule 4 — fewer moving parts, and removes conceptual duplication of the key format between `toKey` and the split).\n\n### Step 2: Initial APP Mass (per-line, focusing on `nextGeneration`)\n\n**Final loop block (the refactoring target):**\n```\nfor (const [cellKey, count] of liveNeighborCounts) {   // loop 5, bindings cellKey+count = 2\n  const isAlive = liveCells.has(cellKey);               // binding 1, invocation .has 2, binding cellKey 1\n  if (count === 3 || (isAlive && count === 2)) {        // conditional 4, === x2 (2 inv=4), && (2), || (2), consts 3,2 (2), bindings count x2 + isAlive (3)\n    const [x, y] = cellKey.split(\",\").map(Number);      // bindings x,y 2, invocations split+map 4, const \",\" 1, binding Number+cellKey 2\n    result.push([x, y]);                                 // invocation push 2, bindings x,y 2\n  }\n}\n```\nLoop-block subtotal: 5 + 2 + (1+2+1) + (4+4+2+2+2+3) + (2+4+1+2) + (2+2) = **43**\n\n### Step 3–4: Apply Rule 2 (reveal intent) + Rule 4 (fewer elements)\n\nStore the coordinate with the count (avoids the string round-trip), and name the rule."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_016mNwsKrm2z2TGQGho4